+## Enabling and disabling CDC after source creation
+
+You don't have to decide on CDC when you first create the source. Open the source's **Configuration** tab to manage CDC at any time:
+
+- **Enable CDC** – choose a management mode (PostHog-managed or self-managed), run the prerequisite check, then enable. PostHog provisions the replication slot (and, in PostHog-managed mode, the publication) and starts the CDC extraction schedule.
+- **Monitor CDC** – see the current management mode, slot and publication names, whether the slot and publication still exist on your database, and the current WAL lag.
+- **Update settings** – change the lag warning and critical thresholds and the automatic slot protection setting.
+- **Disable CDC** – PostHog cancels any running CDC sync, removes the extraction schedule, drops the PostHog-managed replication slot (and publication in PostHog-managed mode; self-managed publications are never touched), and soft-deletes the `_cdc` history tables. CDC tables are paused and must pick a new sync method before they sync again.
+
+Disabling CDC is destructive for CDC state: the replication slot is dropped, so changes made while CDC is disabled can't be recovered from the slot. Re-enabling CDC starts a fresh slot and requires a full resync of the affected tables.
+
 ## WAL lag protection
 
 Replication slots retain WAL until the consumer advances the slot. If PostHog stops consuming changes, retained WAL can grow.
